{"step_index":179,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:36:28Z","thinking":"**Reviewing the Lean challenge**\n\nThe challenge defines a `Candidate` property within `Challenge_2a2555cc.lean`. This candidate involves a graph property where the graph radius is less than or equal to the total domination number for all nontrivial graphs. The goal is to prove this `Candidate` property.\n\n\n**Exploring proof strategy**\n\nConsidering the theorem to prove, the use of classical logic might be beneficial. Focus shifts to finding a central vertex and analyzing its eccentricity within the graph. Investigating the relationship between `GraphCalc.radius` and `SimpleGraph.radius` is critical for linking the definitions.\n\nThe approach of constructing a walk and relating `GraphCalc.radius G ≤ S.card` is promising. Exploring ways to inductively construct the center from a total dominating set and what happens when the induced graph on a total dominating set is connected are potential paths forward.\n\n\n**Analyzing the disconnected components**\n\nThe initial analysis assumes the induced subgraph `G[S]` is connected; this needs extension for the disconnected case. Each connected component of `G[S]` must have size at least 2. The connecting paths between the components in `G` have a maximum length of 3. Trying to find the upper bound on the radius when `G[S]` is disconnected.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"CaseInsensitive":true,"MatchPerLine":true,"Query":"totalDomination","SearchPath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"Search totalDomination in Mathlib"}}]}
