{"step_index":185,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:36:55Z","thinking":"**Analyzing the core theorem statement**\n\nThe goal is to show the radius of a graph is less than or equal to its total domination number. Examining the provided theorem statement, the variables `V`, `_`, `_`, `G`, `_`, `hn`, `hr`, and `ht` are introduced. The challenge is to connect the definition of graph radius and total domination number to the variables provided in the statement. Also, the structure of what was proved in `scratch.lean` might be useful.\n\n\n**Connecting subset condition to radius**\n\nThe `ballWithin_univ_of_subset` lemma implies if a set $S$ is contained within a ball of radius $k$ around a vertex $v$, then the graph's radius is bounded by $k+1$. Considering $k = |S| - 1$, if $S$ is contained within the ball of radius $|S| - 1$ around some vertex $v$, then the radius is at most $|S|$. This is promising if $S$ turns out to be a total dominating set. The problem is whether such a vertex $v$ exists to satisfy $S \\subseteq \\text{ballWithin G (S.card - 1) v}$.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"MatchPerLine":true,"Query":"lemma ","SearchPath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/Diam.lean","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"Grep lemmas in Diam.lean"}}]}
